# End-to-End Encryption for Passwords & Private Items

Implement WhatsApp-style client-side encryption so that **even the database owner / app administrator cannot read** password credentials or private item content. Data is encrypted in the browser before it leaves, and only decrypted in the browser when the owner views or copies it.

## How It Works (High Level)

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant Browser
    participant Server/DB

    Note over Browser: User creates password item
    Browser->>Browser: Generate AES-256-GCM key (per-user)
    Browser->>Browser: Encrypt password + private content
    Browser->>Server/DB: Send ciphertext + IV (server never sees plaintext)
    Note over Server/DB: DB stores only encrypted blobs

    Note over Browser: User copies password
    Browser->>Browser: Retrieve key from IndexedDB
    Browser->>Browser: Decrypt ciphertext → plaintext
    Browser->>Browser: Copy to clipboard
```

### Encryption Strategy

| Aspect | Choice | Rationale |
|--------|--------|-----------|
| Algorithm | **AES-256-GCM** via Web Crypto API | Browser-native, hardware-accelerated, zero dependencies |
| Key derivation | **PBKDF2** from a user-provided "Vault Passphrase" | Only the user knows the passphrase; server never sees it |
| Key storage | **IndexedDB** (session-cached derived key) | Survives page refresh, cleared on logout |
| What's encrypted | `password`, `username`, `content`, `plainText` for `type=password` items AND all fields for `visibility=private` items | Covers all sensitive data |
| DB column | `encrypted_password` already exists; add `encrypted_content`, `encrypted_plain_text`, `encryption_iv`, `encryption_salt` | Backward compatible |

## User Review Required

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Vault Passphrase UX**: Users will be prompted to create a "Vault Passphrase" the first time they save a password or private item. This passphrase is **never sent to the server** — it stays in the browser to derive the encryption key. If the user forgets this passphrase, **their encrypted data is permanently unrecoverable** (just like WhatsApp). We should show a prominent warning about this.

> [!WARNING]
> **Existing data migration**: Any passwords or private items already in the database are currently stored in plaintext. We have two options:
> 1. **Encrypt on next access**: When a user sets their passphrase, automatically encrypt any existing unencrypted items.
> 2. **Leave existing, encrypt new only**: Simpler but inconsistent.
> 
> I recommend option 1 — encrypt existing items when the user first sets their passphrase.

> [!CAUTION]
> **No passphrase recovery**: Since the server never sees the passphrase, there is no "forgot password" flow. If a user loses their passphrase, their encrypted data is **gone forever**. This is the trade-off for true E2E encryption.

## Open Questions

> [!IMPORTANT]
> 1. **Should anonymous (not-logged-in) users also have encryption?** Currently anonymous users can only create public items, so encryption may not apply to them. Confirm this is acceptable.
> 2. **Passphrase prompt frequency**: Should users enter their passphrase once per session (stored in memory/IndexedDB until logout), or every time they want to view a secret? I recommend once per session for better UX.
> 3. **Should `username` and `siteUrl` on password items also be encrypted?** This would hide even the site name from the DB admin but makes search impossible server-side. I recommend encrypting `username` and `password` but keeping `title` and `siteUrl` unencrypted for searchability.

## Proposed Changes

### Crypto Utility Library

#### [NEW] [crypto.ts](file:///c:/Users/misal/OneDrive/Documents/next-framer/lib/vault/crypto.ts)

Client-side encryption/decryption utility using the **Web Crypto API** (zero external dependencies):

- `deriveKey(passphrase: string, salt: Uint8Array): Promise<CryptoKey>` — PBKDF2 key derivation
- `encrypt(plaintext: string, key: CryptoKey): Promise<{ ciphertext: string; iv: string }>` — AES-256-GCM encryption, returns base64-encoded ciphertext + IV
- `decrypt(ciphertext: string, iv: string, key: CryptoKey): Promise<string>` — AES-256-GCM decryption
- `generateSalt(): Uint8Array` — crypto-random salt generation
- `isEncrypted(value: string): boolean` — heuristic to detect if a value is already encrypted (for migration)

